1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,180
$500,000 at 62. That sounds like a lot until you divide it by 30 years.

2
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:13,460
So let's do the division slowly, because the number that comes out is the

3
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,260
one nobody shows you. By the end of this,

4
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:22,460
you'll have three numbers. What $500,000 actually pays you each month.

5
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:25,640
What Social Security adds if you take it at 62.

6
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:29,180
And what's left after the tax man takes his share.

7
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,500
Then we'll do the part most people skip.

8
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:34,640
We'll ask whether that number still works at 82.

9
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,840
Nothing here is advice. It's a worked example with every assumption set

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,220
out loud, so you can swap in your own figures.

11
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:46,260
Here's the person we're using. 62. Single.

12
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:50,640
$500,000 in a traditional 401k or IRA,

13
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,200
which means none of it has been taxed yet.

14
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:56,620
No pension, paid off house, or close to it.

15
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:59,420
That's the whole picture. If yours is different,

16
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:02,560
and it will be, you'll hear exactly where to change the number.

17
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,760
Let's start with the money you saved.

18
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:08,880
There's an old rule of thumb called the 4 % rule.

19
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:13,320
It came out of research in the 1990s that looked at every 30 -year stretch

20
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:16,160
of American market history and asked one question.

21
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:18,740
How much could you have pulled out in year one,

22
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:21,040
raised with inflation every year after,

23
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:24,480
and not run dry before year 30? The answer,

24
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:28,160
in most of those 30 -year windows, was about 4%.

25
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:33,120
4 % of $500,000 is $20,000 a year.

26
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:38,180
Divide by 12. That's $1,667 a month.

27
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:42,400
Hold that. $20,000 a year out of half a million.

28
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:45,200
That's the first number, and for a lot of people,

29
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,980
it's the first shock. The pile looks big.

30
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:52,840
The paycheck it makes does not. And 4 % is the moderate case.

31
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:57,640
Plenty of people who study this now say 3.5 % is the safer place to start

32
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:01,560
because the research covered 30 years and you might need more than that.

33
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:08,480
3.5 % is $17,500 a year. $1,458 a month.

34
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:13,540
So the savings alone pay somewhere between about $1,400 and about $1,700

35
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:16,760
a month. Before tax. Before anything.

36
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,840
Why 30 years? Because that's how long you might need it.

37
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:25,640
The Social Security Administration's own table says a man reaching 65 in

38
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,700
2026 can expect to live to about 84.

39
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:32,420
A woman to about 87. And those are averages.

40
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:35,180
Half the people live longer than the average.

41
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:38,960
Plan for the half you might be in. How to check yours.

42
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:43,980
Take your total retirement savings. Multiply by .04.

43
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:47,770
Then divide by 12. That's your moderate number.

44
00:02:48,430 --> 00:02:52,230
Multiply by .035 instead for the cautious one.

45
00:02:52,810 --> 00:02:57,370
Write both down. We'll need them. Now the second paycheck.

46
00:02:58,010 --> 00:03:00,290
The one you've been paying into since your first job.

47
00:03:01,130 --> 00:03:03,730
Here's the thing about taking Social Security at 62.

48
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:09,670
It works. You can. But the check is permanently smaller and the size of

49
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:14,070
the cut surprises people. For anyone born in 1960 or later,

50
00:03:14,430 --> 00:03:17,950
full retirement age is 67. Claim at 62,

51
00:03:18,370 --> 00:03:23,010
five years early, and Social Security reduces your benefit by 30%.

52
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:27,390
Not for a while. For life. Let's put a figure on it.

53
00:03:27,670 --> 00:03:33,610
The average retired workers check in January 2026 is about $2,071 a month

54
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:37,230
after this year's cost of living increase of 2.8%.

55
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:41,010
So let's give our person a full age benefit of $2,000.

56
00:03:41,570 --> 00:03:45,950
Round. Close to average. Claim at 62 and 30 % comes off.

57
00:03:46,550 --> 00:03:52,010
$2,000 becomes $1,400. $1,400 a month.

58
00:03:52,590 --> 00:03:57,490
That's the second number. One more catch at 62 and it bites anyone who's

59
00:03:57,490 --> 00:04:00,330
still working part -time. There's an earnings test.

60
00:04:00,790 --> 00:04:05,210
For 2026, if you're under full retirement age and you earn more than $24,480

61
00:04:05,210 --> 00:04:11,690
in a year from work, Social Security holds back $1 of benefit for every

62
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:16,810
$2 you earn over that line. Withdrawals from your IRA don't count as earnings.

63
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:21,510
A paycheck does. You get that money back later in a recalculated benefit,

64
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:24,870
but it can hollow out the plan in the years you were counting on it.

65
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:30,930
How to check yours. Go to ssa .gov, sign in to My Social Security and open

66
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:34,230
your statement. It shows your estimated benefit at 62,

67
00:04:41,670 --> 00:04:45,990
at the end of the first. So far we've got two numbers on the table.

68
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:50,430
Savings, about $1,667 a month in the moderate case.

69
00:04:51,170 --> 00:04:56,810
Social Security, $1,400. Add them and you get $3,067 a month.

70
00:04:57,330 --> 00:04:59,310
That is not what lands in your account.

71
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:03,510
Two things come off first. Let's take the smaller one first because it's

72
00:05:03,510 --> 00:05:08,530
the one people get wrong. Tax. Every dollar out of that traditional 401k

73
00:05:08,530 --> 00:05:11,970
is ordinary income because it was never taxed going in.

74
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:15,890
$20,000 of withdrawals is $20,000 of income.

75
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:18,530
Then there's the part almost nobody expects.

76
00:05:19,470 --> 00:05:21,210
Social Security itself can be taxed.

77
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:25,050
The government adds up your other income plus half your Social Security

78
00:05:25,050 --> 00:05:28,490
and calls it provisional income. For a single person,

79
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:33,130
once that total crosses $25,000, some of your benefit becomes taxable.

80
00:05:33,710 --> 00:05:39,390
Let's run it. $20,000 from savings plus half of the $16,800 a year in

81
00:05:39,390 --> 00:05:43,330
Social Security, which is $8,400. Provisional income,

82
00:05:43,730 --> 00:05:48,030
$28,400. That's $3,400 over the line.

83
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:53,510
Half of that, $1,700 of Social Security is now taxable income.

84
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:59,270
Total taxable income, $21,700. Now subtract the standard deduction.

85
00:05:59,710 --> 00:06:07,710
For 2026, single, that's $16,100. What's left is $5,600 and it's all in

86
00:06:07,710 --> 00:06:10,430
the 10 % bracket. Federal tax for the year,

87
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:17,090
$560. About $47 a month. That's a lot smaller than most people fear and

88
00:06:17,090 --> 00:06:19,310
it's worth knowing why. At this income,

89
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:21,470
the standard deduction eats most of it.

90
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:26,410
The tax problem in retirement usually shows up later at 73 when required

91
00:06:26,410 --> 00:06:29,730
minimum distributions start and the government makes you take money out

92
00:06:29,730 --> 00:06:32,310
whether you want it or not. That's its own episode.

93
00:06:33,110 --> 00:06:36,830
State tax is your number, not mine. Some states tax none of this,

94
00:06:37,110 --> 00:06:40,570
some tax all of it. Look yours up. So here's the ledger.

95
00:06:41,850 --> 00:06:47,050
Savings, $1,667. Social Security, $1,400.

96
00:06:47,690 --> 00:06:52,670
Federal tax, minus $47. $3,020 a month.

97
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:58,670
That's the number. $500,000 at 62 with an average Social Security check

98
00:06:58,670 --> 00:07:02,570
comes to about $3,020 a month after federal tax.

99
00:07:03,030 --> 00:07:08,290
In the cautious case at 3.5%, it's about $2,843.

100
00:07:08,810 --> 00:07:13,590
Now the item that isn't on that ledger and it's the big one at 62.

101
00:07:14,210 --> 00:07:17,850
Health insurance. Medicare doesn't start until 65.

102
00:07:18,490 --> 00:07:22,570
That's three years you're buying your own coverage and the premium depends

103
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:25,990
on your income and your state. So I'm not going to make one up.

104
00:07:26,550 --> 00:07:30,510
Go to healthcare .gov, put in your zip code and the income we just calculated

105
00:07:30,510 --> 00:07:34,770
and get your real quote. Then subtract it from 3,020.

106
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:37,950
For some people, that line changes everything.

107
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:42,070
How to check yours. Add your two numbers from before.

108
00:07:42,550 --> 00:07:46,610
Then look up the standard deduction for your filing status and run the provisional

109
00:07:46,610 --> 00:07:49,330
income test with your own Social Security figure.

110
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:55,170
It's four lines of arithmetic. If you'd rather a CPA or a fee only fiduciary

111
00:07:55,170 --> 00:07:58,950
can do the whole thing in an hour with your actual statements in front of

112
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:02,590
them. And that's the one time in this video I'll say that.

113
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:07,050
$3,020 a month. Does it still work at 82?

114
00:08:07,950 --> 00:08:10,690
Here's the problem. That number is in $20,

115
00:08:10,710 --> 00:08:15,790
$26. At 3 % inflation, which is roughly the long run average,

116
00:08:16,190 --> 00:08:23,150
20 years from now, $3,020 buys what about $1,670 buys today.

117
00:08:23,690 --> 00:08:29,230
At 30 years, about $1,240. Social Security fixes part of that for you.

118
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:31,770
It gets a cost of living increase most years.

119
00:08:32,330 --> 00:08:37,510
This year's was 2.8%. The 4 % rule assumes you raise your withdrawal with

120
00:08:37,510 --> 00:08:40,990
inflation too. But that's the assumption doing the heavy lifting.

121
00:08:41,430 --> 00:08:43,490
And it's the one that breaks in a bad decade.

122
00:08:44,050 --> 00:08:47,610
If the market falls hard in your first five years while you're pulling $20,000

123
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:51,830
a year out, the math from the 1990s research starts to fail.

124
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:54,870
That's why the cautious people say three and a half.

125
00:08:55,610 --> 00:08:58,230
So let's run one alternative. Not a recommendation,

126
00:08:58,850 --> 00:09:02,790
just the other number. What if this person waits on Social Security until

127
00:09:02,790 --> 00:09:06,210
67 and lives on savings alone for five years?

128
00:09:06,710 --> 00:09:09,510
The check at 67 is the full $2,000,

129
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:14,330
not $1,400. $600 a month more. For life.

130
00:09:14,890 --> 00:09:17,210
With cost of living increases on the bigger figure.

131
00:09:17,770 --> 00:09:21,130
The cost is five years of Social Security they didn't collect.

132
00:09:21,830 --> 00:09:25,690
$1,400 a month, 60 months, $84,000.

133
00:09:26,290 --> 00:09:29,050
That has to come out of the $500,000 instead.

134
00:09:29,510 --> 00:09:32,230
On top of the $20,000 a year they were already taking.

135
00:09:32,970 --> 00:09:38,650
Divide $84,000 by the $600 a month they gain and it takes about 140 months

136
00:09:38,650 --> 00:09:44,530
to catch up. That's about age 79. Live past 79 and waiting paid more.

137
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:48,630
Don't and claiming early did. Neither answer is right.

138
00:09:49,110 --> 00:09:52,070
One of them is right for you and it depends on your health,

139
00:09:52,430 --> 00:09:54,390
your savings and whether you're still working.

140
00:09:54,970 --> 00:09:59,070
What matters is that you saw both numbers before you signed anything because

141
00:09:59,070 --> 00:10:02,970
the claiming decision is one of the few in retirement you can't take back.

142
00:10:03,690 --> 00:10:09,170
How to check yours. On that same Social Security statement take the 67 figure

143
00:10:09,170 --> 00:10:13,550
minus the 62 figure. Multiply the 62 figure by 60.

144
00:10:14,070 --> 00:10:17,590
Divide the second by the first. That's your break even in months.

145
00:10:18,050 --> 00:10:21,230
Count it from 67 because that's when the bigger check starts.

146
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:28,350
So three numbers. Half a million dollars pays about $1,700 a month if you

147
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:34,370
want it to last 30 years. Social Security at 62 adds about $1,400 and takes

148
00:10:34,370 --> 00:10:37,030
30 % off your check forever to do it.

149
00:10:37,450 --> 00:10:43,010
After federal tax that's about $3,020 a month before health insurance,

150
00:10:43,350 --> 00:10:46,830
before your state, before inflation gets its 30 years.

151
00:10:47,510 --> 00:10:51,410
One thing to do this week. Pull your Social Security statement and your

152
00:10:51,410 --> 00:10:55,810
latest 401k balance and put the two numbers next to each other on one sheet

153
00:10:55,810 --> 00:10:59,190
of paper. That sheet is the beginning of the whole plan.

154
00:10:59,710 --> 00:11:01,790
If you want the next number work the same way,

155
00:11:02,210 --> 00:11:05,550
subscribe. Friday's episode is the other side of this one.

156
00:11:05,810 --> 00:11:10,170
A $40,000 pension against the lump sum they offer you to give it up.

157
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:13,980
Will your money last as long as you do?

158
00:11:14,730 --> 00:11:17,080
Run your own number. See you Friday.
